# Keep the upstream sysupgrade files out of the writable layer's upper.
#
# /lib/upgrade is also the path of this project's own upgrade adapter
# (platform/lib/upgrade/platform.sh, installed by the platform layer). The running root is
# an overlayfs composition of the platform layer over @sys, and a file the user's writable
# layer (upper) carries wins over both. An `apk upgrade` that rewrites the upstream
# /lib/upgrade/* files would therefore put an upper copy in front of the platform layer:
# the next ab-upgrade sources upstream's script, which has no platform_upgrade_begin, and
# the update path fails after every validation step has already passed.
#
# A leading `+` protects a path only when the visible file differs from the one recorded
# in the apk database (`apk-protected_paths(5)`, 2025-03-10: "Protect matching paths only
# if the file's checksum does not match the one in the apk database"; `!` is the
# unconditional form). That condition is a fact that can be asked for, and only
# `platform.sh` satisfies it here: the platform layer ships its own platform.sh, while
# fwtool.sh, stage2 and tar.sh are upstream's files with nothing shadowing them. So this
# file is one rule that will fire and three that will not, and the three are listed on
# purpose - they are the upstream sysupgrade interface this project has to notice if
# upstream ever starts replacing one of them.
#
# What the rule buys: an apk that rewrites a protected path writes the package's copy as
# `<name>.apk-new` beside it, the platform layer keeps precedence, and apk's database
# still upgrades normally. What the three no-op rules buy: nothing today, and that is
# fine - apk should keep those three in step with upstream. Do not turn them into `!`
# rules to make the file look symmetric: that would freeze upstream fixes out.
#
# The condition is checked on a device by scripts/device/apk-protection-probe.sh, and the
# proof (a real transaction writing `*.apk-new`) needs an upgrade candidate; a baseline
# that is already newest has none, so the probe is re-run around the next real upgrade.
# Do not remove these rules without a device check: the cleanup in platform_copy_rootfs
# only repairs a snapshot that already carries a shadow, and this file is what stops the
# shadow from being created again.
#
# The list below is the exception, not the general rule: it names the four paths of
# upstream's sysupgrade interface, which all belong to base-files. Every other layer file
# that an apk package owns is covered by ab-platform-shadow.list, which the build derives
# from the layer's own file list intersected with the root filesystem's apk database (see
# scripts/ci/assemble.sh). Keep that derivation, not this file, as the place to add a
# path: a hand-written entry here can only be correct until the next upstream package
# changes which files it owns.
+lib/upgrade/platform.sh
+lib/upgrade/fwtool.sh
+lib/upgrade/stage2
+lib/upgrade/tar.sh
